{:edb/db-before-t 1436 :edb/db-after-t 1437 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534749 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:47:32.045Z" 13194139534749 true] [17592186192304 1145 "Recall that the principal cube root of unity is\n\n$$\nz=e^{2\\pi\\text{i}/3}\n$$\n\nand that any cube root of unity is of the form $z^k$ for $k=0,1,2$. Also, we have $z^3=1$.\n\nTherefore, the product of all three roots is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{0} \\cdot z^{1} \\cdot z^{2} &= z^{0 + 1 + 2} \\\\\n&= z^{3} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534749 false] [17592186192304 1145 "Recall that the principal cube root of unity is \n\n$$\nz=e^{2\\pi\\text{i}/3},\n$$\n\nand that any cube root of unity is of the form $z^k$ for $k=0,1,2.$ Also, we have $z^3=1.$\n\nTherefore, the product of all three roots is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{0}\\cdot {z}^{1}\\cdot {z}^{2} & ={z}^{0\\,+\\,1\\,+\\,2} \\\\  & ={z}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534749 true] [17592186192307 1100 "Let $z$ be a $7$th root of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $z^7 = 1$\n\n- $(z+z)^7 = 1$\n\n- $z+z=2z$ is a $7$th root of unity" 13194139534749 true] [17592186192307 1100 "Let $z$ be a $7$th root of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $z^7 = 1$\n2. $(z+z)^7 = 1$\n3. $z+z=2z$ is a $7$th root of unity" 13194139534749 false] [17592186192307 1145 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $z$ is a $7$th root of unity, then (by definition), we have that\n$z^7=1$.\n- Statement II is false. We have\n$(z + z)^{7}|= (2z)^{7}; = 2^{7} \\cdot z^{7}; = 128 \\cdot 1; = 128; \\ne 1$.\n- Statement III is false. From statement II, we have that $(z+z)^7 \\neq 1$, which means that $z+z=2z$ is *not* a $7$th root of unity. In general, the sum of two $n$th roots of unity is *not* an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534749 false] [17592186192307 1145 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $z$ is a $7$th root of unity, then (by definition), we have that \n\n$$\nz^7=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+z)^{7} & =(2z)^{7} \\\\  & ={2}^{7}\\cdot {z}^{7} \\\\  & =128\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =128 \\\\  & \\ne 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. From statement II, we have that $(z+z)^7 \\neq 1,$ which means that $z+z=2z$ is *not* a $7$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the sum of two $n$th roots of unity is *not* an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534749 true] [17592186192308 1100 "The $12$th root of unity $e^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}$ can be written as\n$e^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3} = z^p$,\nwhere $z$ is the principal $12$th root of unity. What is the value of $p$?" 13194139534749 false] [17592186192308 1100 "The $12$th root of unity $e^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}$ can be written as \n\n$$\ne^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3} = z^p,\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ is the principal $12$th root of unity. What is the value of $p?$" 13194139534749 true] [17592186192308 1145 "Recall that the $12$th roots of unity can be written in the form\n\n$$\ne^{2 k\\pi \\text{i} / 12}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nk=0,1,\\ldots,11\n$$\n\nWe obtain the *principal* $12$th root of unity by substituting $k=1{:}$\n\n$$\nz= e^{2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\pi \\text{i} / 12} = {\\color{blue}e^{\\pi \\text{i} / 6}}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given root of unity as a power of the principal root as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{2π\\textrm{i}/3} &= e^{(2 \\cdot 2π)\\textrm{i}/(2 \\cdot 3)} \\\\\n&= e^{4π\\textrm{i}/6} \\\\\n&= e^{4 \\cdot π\\textrm{i}/6} \\\\\n&= (e^{π\\textrm{i}/6})^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\np={\\color{red}4}\n$$" 13194139534749 false] [17592186192308 1145 "Recall that the $12$th roots of unity can be written in the form\n\n$$\ne^{2 k\\pi \\text{i} / 12}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=0,1,\\ldots,11.$ \n\nWe obtain the *principal* $12$th root of unity by substituting $k=1{:}$\n\n$$\nz= e^{2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\pi \\text{i} / 12} = {\\color{blue}e^{\\pi \\text{i} / 6}}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given root of unity as a power of the principal root as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3} & ={e}^{(2\\cdot 2\\pi )\\text{i}/(2\\cdot 3)} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/6} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\cdot \\pi \\text{i}/6} \\\\  & =({e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/6})^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p={\\color{red}4}.$" 13194139534749 true] [17592186192628 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307476 13194139534749 true] [17592186192628 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307483 13194139534749 true] [17592186192628 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307525 13194139534749 true] [17592186192628 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307528 13194139534749 true] [17592186192628 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307531 13194139534749 true] [17592186192629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307490 13194139534749 true] [17592186192629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307497 13194139534749 true] [17592186192629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307504 13194139534749 true] [17592186192629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307511 13194139534749 true] [17592186192629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307518 13194139534749 true] [17592186192630 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307535 13194139534749 true] [17592186192630 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307539 13194139534749 true] [17592186192630 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307543 13194139534749 true] [17592186192630 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307546 13194139534749 true] [17592186192630 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307549 13194139534749 true] [17592186307476 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307477 13194139534749 true] [17592186307476 1089 "q-162337" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307476 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534749 true] [17592186307476 1100 "The cube root of unity ${e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}={z}^{p},\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ is the principal cube root of unity. What is the value of $p?$" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307476 1113 #uuid "fef237eb-4fb5-56f8-847a-b19578c620ae" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307476 1145 "Recall that any cube root of unity can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=0,1,2.$ \n\nWe obtain the *principal* cube root of unity by substituting $k=1:$\n\n$$\nz={e}^{2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi \\text{i}/3}={e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given root of unity as a power of the principal root as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3} & =({e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2.$" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307478 13194139534749 true] [17592186307477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307479 13194139534749 true] [17592186307477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307480 13194139534749 true] [17592186307477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307481 13194139534749 true] [17592186307477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307482 13194139534749 true] [17592186307477 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534749 true] [17592186307477 1041 "selection" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307477 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307479 13194139534749 true] [17592186307477 1110 #uuid "483d69a7-783e-51a0-ac3a-70450403dc5f" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307478 1034 "3" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307478 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534749 true] [17592186307478 1111 #uuid "a0eb588c-8129-5e7a-b5ab-a4d4d113e348" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307479 1034 "2" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534749 true] [17592186307479 1111 #uuid "d626c820-bf6d-5112-bd1c-fbbe0fea09ae" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307480 1034 "4" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307480 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534749 true] [17592186307480 1111 #uuid "67872927-2151-5151-b615-9b447aee2bfc" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307481 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307481 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534749 true] [17592186307481 1111 #uuid "26fbcfd8-ce0e-5947-924e-198ffe277c6a" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307482 1034 "5" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307482 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534749 true] [17592186307482 1111 #uuid "2664b6a0-466b-516f-b227-357acb0983a8" 13194139534749 true] [17592186307483 1017 #e